---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import CommandOutput from '../../../components/CommandOutput.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="CLI webhooks" activeNav="/cli/integrations/webhooks">
  <h1>Webhooks</h1>
  <p>
    A webhook endpoint receives platform events over HTTP: builds finishing, artifacts arriving,
    identifiers changing. Deliveries are signed, retried, and recorded, so the CLI can tell you
    not just what you subscribed to but whether it actually arrived.
  </p>
  <p>
    For payload formats and signature verification see the
    <a href="/guides/webhooks">Webhooks guide</a>. This page is about managing endpoints from
    the command line.
  </p>

  <h2>Commands</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl webhooks list
appmctl webhooks get    WEBHOOK_ID
appmctl webhooks create --name NAME --url URL --events JSON [filters]
appmctl webhooks update WEBHOOK_ID [--name NAME] [--url URL] [--events JSON] [filters]
appmctl webhooks delete WEBHOOK_ID
appmctl webhooks test   WEBHOOK_ID
appmctl webhooks rotate WEBHOOK_ID
appmctl webhooks deliveries WEBHOOK_ID
appmctl webhooks redeliver --webhook-id ID DELIVERY_ID`} />

  <h3>List</h3>
  <CommandOutput command="appmctl webhooks list" capture="webhooks-list" />
  <p>
    <code>enabled</code> follows the state of the endpoint's integration. A disabled integration
    stops deliveries without deleting the endpoint or its history.
  </p>

  <h3>Show one endpoint</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl webhooks get 019fc795-6498-7243-a2ba-f97ef4cee99a

# id:                     019fc795-6498-7243-a2ba-f97ef4cee99a
# name:                   Release notifier
# url:                    https://hooks.acme.example/app-manager
# events:                 build.created, build.completed, artifact.created
# enabled:                true
# secret_hint:            whse…000
# consecutive_failures:   0
# last_delivered_at:      2026-08-03T12:30:00+00:00`} />
  <p>
    <code>consecutive_failures</code> is the health signal to watch. The secret itself is never
    returned — only a hint, enough to tell two secrets apart in a log.
  </p>

  <h3>Create and update</h3>
  <p>
    <code>--name</code>, <code>--url</code>, and <code>--events</code> are all required:
    the API rejects an endpoint without a name or with an empty subscription. Event names
    come from a fixed catalog — there is no <code>build.failed</code>, because a finished
    build reports through <code>build.completed</code> and carries its own status.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl webhooks create \\
  --name 'Release notifier' \\
  --url https://hooks.acme.example/app-manager \\
  --events '["build.created","build.completed","artifact.created"]'

# Subscriptions are replaced, not merged
appmctl webhooks update 019fc795-6498-7243-a2ba-f97ef4cee99a \\
  --events '["build.completed"]'

appmctl webhooks delete 019fc795-6498-7243-a2ba-f97ef4cee99a`} />

  <h3>Filtering what an endpoint receives</h3>
  <p>
    Without filters an endpoint receives every subscribed event in the team. Filters narrow
    that by app, build status, or build tag, and each flag is repeatable:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl webhooks create \\
  --name 'Android releases' \\
  --url https://hooks.acme.example/android \\
  --events '["build.completed"]' \\
  --app customer-app \\
  --build-status completed \\
  --tag android`} />
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Filter</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>--app SLUG</code></td><td>Only events for that app. The slug is resolved to an app id, which costs one extra request.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--app-id UUID</code></td><td>The same filter when you already hold the id.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--build-status STATUS</code></td><td>Only builds reaching that status</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--tag TAG</code></td><td>Only builds carrying that tag</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    On <code>update</code>, filters replace the stored set rather than merging into it, the
    same way <code>--events</code> does.
  </p>

  <h2>Testing an endpoint</h2>
  <p>
    <code>test</code> sends a synthetic delivery so you can verify a receiver end to end before
    a real build depends on it:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl webhooks test 019fc795-6498-7243-a2ba-f97ef4cee99a`} />
  <p>
    A test delivery appears in <code>deliveries</code> like any other, which is the point: if the
    test does not show up there, the problem is on this side, and if it shows up as failed, the
    problem is at your receiver.
  </p>

  <h2>Rotating the signing secret</h2>
  <p>
    <code>rotate</code> issues a new HMAC secret and returns it once. It is not retrievable
    afterwards, so capture it in the same step that stores it:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl --json webhooks rotate 019fc795-6498-7243-a2ba-f97ef4cee99a |
  jq -r '.data.secret'`} />
  <p>
    Rotation takes effect immediately: deliveries signed with the old secret stop verifying at
    your receiver the moment it happens. Deploy the new secret to the receiver first if you
    cannot tolerate a gap, or accept a brief window of rejected deliveries and let the retry
    schedule cover it.
  </p>

  <h2>Inspecting deliveries</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl webhooks deliveries 019fc795-6498-7243-a2ba-f97ef4cee99a

# id       event            status     http  attempts  delivered_at
# -------  ---------------  ---------  ----  --------  ------------------------
# del-001  build.completed  delivered  200   1         2026-08-03T12:30:00+00:00
# del-002  build.created    exhausted  500   5`} />
  <p>
    <code>status</code> is the platform's own verdict, <code>http</code> the receiver's response
    code, and <code>attempts</code> how many tries it took. A delivery with no
    <code>delivered_at</code> never succeeded.
  </p>
  <p>Full details, including the response preview and the last error, come from <code>--json</code>:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl --json webhooks deliveries "$WH" |
  jq '.data[] | select(.status == "failed") | {id, event_type, response_status, last_error, next_attempt_at}'`} />

  <h3>Redelivering</h3>
  <p>
    After fixing a receiver, replay what it missed. <code>redeliver</code> needs both ids,
    since a delivery id only means something in the context of its endpoint:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl webhooks redeliver --webhook-id "$WH" del-002

# Replay everything that failed
appmctl --json webhooks deliveries "$WH" |
  jq -r '.data[] | select(.status == "failed") | .id' |
  while read -r delivery; do
    appmctl webhooks redeliver --webhook-id "$WH" "$delivery"
  done`} />
  <p>
    A redelivery is a new delivery that records what it replays, so the original attempt stays in
    the history rather than being overwritten.
  </p>

  <h2>Watching endpoint health in CI</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# Fail a monitoring job when any endpoint is failing repeatedly
appmctl --json webhooks list |
  jq -e '[.data[] | select(.consecutive_failures > 3)] | length == 0' >/dev/null ||
  { echo 'a webhook endpoint is failing repeatedly' >&2; exit 1; }`} />

  <h2>Scopes</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Command</th><th>Scope</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>list</code>, <code>get</code></td><td><code>webhooks:read</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>test</code>, <code>rotate</code></td><td><code>webhooks:write</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>deliveries</code></td><td><code>deliveries:read</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>redeliver</code></td><td><code>webhooks:write</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    A monitoring key needs <code>webhooks:read</code> and <code>deliveries:read</code> only —
    keep <code>webhooks:write</code>, which can rotate a secret, out of it.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
